   =20
    <div class=3D"box box2">
        <main>
            <section class=3D"section1">
                <img src=3D"file:///Users/solexbaby/Desktop/Scrimba-%20RWD/=
Practice%205/image3.jpg">
                <p class=3D"date">Charlottenburg Palace =E2=80=94 Echoes of=
 Royal Berlin<br> =E2=80=9CWhere Berlin=E2=80=99s royal past still whispers=
 through gilded halls and quiet gardens.=E2=80=9D</p>
                <h2>History &amp; Landmarks</h2>
                <p>Berlin=E2=80=99s history is never far from sight=E2=80=
=94it's written into its architecture, layered through its neighborhoods,=
=20
                and even whispered through the quiet moments between busy s=
treets. Exploring the city=E2=80=99s historic landmarks has always=20
                felt, to me, like uncovering chapters of a book that=E2=80=
=99s still being written. Each visit deepens the story,=20
                revealing new details that change how I understand not just=
 Berlin, but the world around it.</p>
                <img src=3D"file:///Users/solexbaby/Desktop/Scrimba-%20RWD/=
Practice%205/image6.jpg">
                <p class=3D"date">Brandenburg Gate =E2=80=94 Where History =
Opens Its Arms<br> =E2=80=9CStanding beneath its arches, I feel Berlin=E2=
=80=99s past and present meeting in the same breath.=E2=80=9D</p>
                <p>My journey often begins at the Brandenburg Gate, a symbo=
l that used to divide and now defines unity.=20
                I still remember the first time I saw it at sunrise=E2=80=
=94the plaza nearly empty, the soft light catching the columns just right.=
=20
                It felt like standing in a place where history inhaled and =
exhaled. From there, I=E2=80=99ll wander toward Pariser Platz and let the a=
tmosphere shift from solemn to lively,=20
                a reminder of how cities heal.</p>
                <img src=3D"file:///Users/solexbaby/Desktop/Scrimba-%20RWD/=
Practice%205/image5.jpg">
                <p class=3D"date">Memorial of Silence =E2=80=94 Paths of Me=
mory<br> =E2=80=9CWalking through these shifting corridors reminds me how h=
istory is felt, not just learned.=E2=80=9D</p>
                <p>Not far away, the Memorial to the Murdered Jews of Europ=
e brings a completely different kind of reflection.=20
                Walking among the concrete slabs is disorienting, emotional=
ly heavy, yet essential. It=E2=80=99s a space that doesn=E2=80=99t tell you=
=20
                what to feel=E2=80=94it simply holds space for whatever tru=
th you bring to it. I often see visitors fall silent, not out of obligation=
, but instinctively.=20
                It=E2=80=99s one of those places where history feels close =
enough to touch.</p>
                <p>Of course, no exploration is complete without tracing th=
e path of the Berlin Wall. The East Side Gallery is one of my personal favo=
rites=E2=80=94not=20
                just because it's the longest open-air gallery in the world=
, but because it captures the moment Berlin turned pain into expression. Ea=
ch mural,=20
                from hopeful doves to political satire, feels like a heartb=
eat. I love visiting early in the morning, when the colors are vibrant and =
the crowds haven=E2=80=99t begun to gather.=20
                If you look closely, you=E2=80=99ll notice how some murals =
have been restored multiple times=E2=80=94evidence that Berlin protects the=
 symbols of its own rebirth.</p>
                <img src=3D"file:///Users/solexbaby/Desktop/Scrimba-%20RWD/=
Practice%205/image7.jpg">
                <p class=3D"date">East Side Gallery =E2=80=94 Walls That Le=
arned to Speak<br> =E2=80=9CHere, the scars of the past turn into bold colo=
rs, carrying stories of hope along the river.=E2=80=9D</p>
                <p>Taking a detour into museums opens another doorway into =
the past. Museum Island is like a world of its own: five museums,=20
                each holding artifacts that span from ancient civilizations=
 to the 19th century. Pergamon altar fragments, intricate Islamic art,=20
                Greek statues=E2=80=94every room feels like a portal. I som=
etimes spend hours wandering the quieter galleries, letting the weight of c=
enturies settle in.=20
                It=E2=80=99s one of the few places where the global and the=
 local histories merge naturally.</p>
                <p>But what I love most about Berlin=E2=80=99s landmarks is=
 that they aren=E2=80=99t isolated. They live in the rhythm of the city. A =
former checkpoint becomes a tourist hub;=20
                a preserved watchtower stands quietly next to new construct=
ion; meaningful plaques appear on random building corners, marking events y=
ou=E2=80=99d never know happened.=20
                The past blends with the present, unforced and unhidden.</p=
>
                <p>If you walk through Berlin long enough, you start to fee=
l that the city isn=E2=80=99t defined by what happened here=E2=80=94it=E2=
=80=99s defined by how it remembers, rebuilds, and retells its stories.=20
                And in those stories, there=E2=80=99s always an invitation:=
 history here doesn=E2=80=99t stay behind glass=E2=80=94it invites you to s=
tand inside it.</p>
            </section>     =20
        </main>
